Decomposition boosts recall (per-type recall reaches 0.586 versus single-pass 0.368) but devastates precision (0.194 versus 0.563). When the same text is presented to the model $k$ times with different type subsets, each pass independently generates entity predictions. Because LLM outputs are not calibrated across passes, the union merge step accumulates overlapping and contradictory predictions, inflating false positives.

Our v2 merge ranks competing predictions by the LLM's self-reported confidence and retains only the highest-scoring candidate for each span. Confidence gating improves precision (sdgd\_zero: 0.210$\to$0.324; random\_batch: 0.249$\to$0.331), but at the cost of disproportionate recall loss (0.314$\to$0.191; 0.446$\to$0.276), yielding lower F1 in both modes (sdgd\_zero: 0.251$\to$0.241; random\_batch: 0.320$\to$0.301).

\section{Factorial Analysis}
\label{sec:appendix_factorial}

We conducted a within-dataset $2\!\times\!2$ factorial analysis (count $\times$ BCS) on Few-NERD to test whether the two degradation channels exhibit a statistical interaction. The interaction term was directionally consistent but not statistically significant ($z = -0.67$, $p = 0.50$), likely underpowered due to BCS range compression in high-count schemas. The $3.4\times$ amplification of confusability effects across FewRel schema scales (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) provides indirect evidence that confusability effects compound with schema size, but a formal factorial test on FewRel was not conducted because the BCS-controlled design varies confusability within fixed count levels rather than crossing both factors.

\end{document}

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Boundary conditions.}
The two-channel framework applies most cleanly to cross-domain schemas where types span multiple semantic clusters. For same-domain schemas (e.g., MAVEN's FrameNet-derived types), the centrality confound (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}) complicates BCS-level analysis, though the pairwise SCS signal remains intact. Practitioners should use BCS as a schema difficulty predictor for cross-domain ontologies, and pairwise SCS directly---or with centrality factored out---for same-domain schemas.

\paragraph{The 51\% floor.}
High-BCS accuracy converges to $\approx$51\% on FewRel regardless of model size (8B vs.\ 32B), well above random guessing (12.5\% for 8-way classification). This suggests that under high confusability, models can still resolve a ``confusion subgroup'' of easily distinguishable relations but fail on the tightly clustered remainder. The floor's model-invariance indicates a task-inherent difficulty ceiling that scaling alone cannot overcome.

\paragraph{Merge amplification and future directions.}
Our SDGD experiments show that naive decompose-then-merge pipelines fail because union merge accumulates contradictory predictions across passes. Three directions may address this: (1)~calibration-aware merge, where cross-batch confidence scores are normalized before aggregation; (2)~joint decoding, where the model sees all types but decodes with confusability-aware constraints; and (3)~hierarchical extraction, processing coarse-grained types first and refining within clusters.

\paragraph{Schema design implications.}
Our results suggest concrete guidelines: monitor BCS when assembling extraction schemas---high-BCS batches are unreliable regardless of model; for large ontologies, prefer cross-domain type groupings that minimize intra-batch similarity; when decomposition is necessary, invest in cross-batch calibration rather than decomposition quality, since merge amplification---not partitioning---is the bottleneck.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

